ord-257bm-i11a"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.record-257bm-i11a"],"id":"long.record-257bm-i11a","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 6.100 (Small remainder forces the greedy word)","locator":"Long record Theorem 6.100, p. 72, label record:257bm-i11a","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Let $g\\ge1$ and let $w_1,\\ldots,w_m$ be nonnegative integer weights satisfying\n$$\nw_i\\ge g+\\sum_{j>i}w_j\\qquad(1\\le i\\le m).\n$$\nFix an integer capacity $C\\ge0$. The greedy word $\\gamma$ is obtained by taking $w_i$ exactly when it does not exceed the remaining capacity. For an admissible Boolean word $\\varepsilon$, meaning $\\sum_i\\varepsilon_iw_i\\le C$, one has\n$$\nC-\\sum_i\\varepsilon_iw_i<g\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\varepsilon=\\gamma\\ \\text{and}\\\n C-\\sum_i\\gamma_iw_i<g.\n$$\nIndeed, two different words have sums separated by at least $g$, as seen at their first differing index; the greedy sum is the largest admissible sum. Thus there is at most one admissible word with remainder below $g$, and any such word is greedy. The greedy word alone need not have remainder below $g$: for weights $(5)$, gap $g=2$ and capacity $C=3$, its remainder is $3$.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:57133a32273c39019ea3f4ba3de79281b7177858848a9345731e250a68a8c176/nodes/long.record-257bm-i11a","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:57133a32273c39019ea3f4ba3de79281b7177858848a9345731e250a68a8c176/nodes/long.record-257bm-i11a","id":"sha256:57133a32273c39019ea3f4ba3de79281b7177858848a9345731e250a68a8c176/edges/%5B%22long.record-257bm-i11a%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.BooleanMobiusGreedyReduction.remainder_lt_gap_iff_eq_integerGreedyBits%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:57133a32273c39019ea3f4ba3de79281b7177858848a9345731e250a68a8c176/edges/%5B%22long.record-257bm-i11a%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.BooleanMobiusGreedyReduction.remainder_lt_gap_iff_eq_integerGreedyBits%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","semantic